# Jeevs Edition — Batch 02: Anemia I · Impaired Production
# Source: OMK_2A_Heme_Summative_3_Objective_Review.html §4 (iron), §5 (B12/folate), §6 (AOCD)
# Options are alphabetised; "correct" is the 0-based index AFTER alphabetisation.
Q = []

def q(stem, choices, correct, explanation, wrong, eli5, image=None, imcap=None, exim=None, excap=None):
    d = {"stem": stem, "choices": choices, "correct": correct, "explanation": explanation,
         "wrongExplanations": wrong, "eli5": eli5}
    if image: d["image"] = image; d["imageCaption"] = imcap
    if exim: d["explanationImage"] = exim; d["explanationImageCaption"] = excap
    Q.append(d)

# ── 1. Iron deficiency in an adult man → find the leak (§4) ────────────────
q("A 61-year-old man comes to the office because of a 4-month history of fatigue and shortness of breath when climbing stairs. He has no abdominal pain, change in bowel habits, or visible blood in his stool. He takes no medications, including over-the-counter analgesics. He eats a varied diet that includes red meat. Physical examination shows conjunctival pallor; the abdomen is soft and nontender. A single test of the stool for occult blood is negative.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 10.4 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\nHematocrit 31% (N=41%–53%)\nMean corpuscular volume 72 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nRed cell distribution width 17.9% (N=11.5%–14.5%)\nReticulocyte count 0.7% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\nPlatelet count 468,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\nSerum iron 28 µg/dL (N=65–175 µg/dL)\nTotal iron-binding capacity 452 µg/dL (N=250–400 µg/dL)\nSerum ferritin 7 ng/mL (N=20–250 ng/mL)\nTransferrin saturation 6% (N=20%–50%)\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate next step in diagnosis?",
  ["A. Bidirectional endoscopy (colonoscopy and esophagogastroduodenoscopy)", "B. Bone marrow aspiration with Prussian blue stain", "C. Hemoglobin electrophoresis", "D. Serum erythropoietin concentration", "E. Serum lead concentration"],
  0,
  "Every value in the panel is one coherent row for IRON DEFICIENCY: microcytosis, a high red cell distribution width, low serum iron, HIGH total iron-binding capacity, a ferritin of 7 ng/mL, a transferrin saturation of 6%, and the reactive thrombocytosis that often accompanies it. The diagnosis is made. The question is where the iron went.\n\nThe review file calls this the single highest-yield sentence in the anemia lectures: iron deficiency in an ADULT MALE or a POSTMENOPAUSAL WOMAN is gastrointestinal blood loss until proven otherwise. He has no dietary explanation and no menstrual losses, so the leak must be found. Colorectal adenocarcinoma can present with iron deficiency and nothing else → the patient bleeds slowly into the stool without noticing → the loss is chronic and insidious. Iron deficiency is a symptom, not a final diagnosis.\n\nThe decoy is the NEGATIVE stool occult blood test. Bleeding from a colonic or gastric lesion is intermittent, so a single negative test does not exclude a gastrointestinal source. It does not change the need for endoscopic evaluation of both the upper and lower tract.",
  {"1": "A marrow iron stain is the historical gold standard for iron stores, and would be the answer if the iron studies were contradictory — for example a normal ferritin in an inflamed patient. Here the ferritin of 7 ng/mL already proves the stores are empty, so an invasive procedure adds nothing and still does not find the bleeding source.",
   "2": "Hemoglobin electrophoresis would be the next step for microcytosis with NORMAL iron studies, a normal or high red cell count, and a normal red cell distribution width — the thalassemia trait profile. A ferritin of 7 ng/mL excludes that picture.",
   "3": "Erythropoietin measurement helps with unexplained hypoproliferative anemia, particularly in chronic kidney disease, or in the workup of polycythemia. It would not be informative once a definite iron deficiency has been demonstrated.",
   "4": "Lead poisoning also gives a microcytic anemia, but with basophilic stippling, abdominal pain or neurologic symptoms, an exposure history, and iron studies that are not deficient. This man has none of those, and his ferritin is profoundly low."},
  "His blood is low on iron, and in a grown man iron does not just disappear — it leaks out as blood, usually from the gut. One clean stool test does not rule that out, so you look inside with a camera.",
  exim="fig_ida_progression",
  excap="Laboratory changes with progressive iron deficiency: ferritin falls first, then serum iron and transferrin saturation, and only last do the MCV and hemoglobin fall.")

# ── 2. Toddler on cow's milk — why the cells are small (§4) ────────────────
q("An 18-month-old boy is brought to the office by his mother because of pallor and irritability for 2 months. He drinks approximately 1.2 L (40 oz) of whole cow's milk daily and eats very little solid food. He was born at term and has no history of serious illness. He takes no medications. Physical examination shows pallor of the palmar creases and conjunctivae; there is no hepatosplenomegaly or jaundice.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 8.1 g/dL (N=11.0–13.5 g/dL for age)\nHematocrit 25% (N=33%–39% for age)\nMean corpuscular volume 63 µm3 (N=70–86 µm3 for age)\nRed cell distribution width 18.6% (N=11.5%–14.5%)\nReticulocyte count 0.8% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\nPlatelet count 512,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\n\nA peripheral blood smear is shown.\n\nWhich of the following best explains the small size of this patient's erythrocytes?",
  ["A. Accelerated splenic removal of membrane from circulating erythrocytes", "B. Additional divisions of erythroid precursors before the hemoglobin target is reached", "C. Impaired globin chain synthesis with precipitation of unpaired chains", "D. Loss of intracellular water through an abnormal cation leak", "E. Premature release of immature erythrocytes from the marrow"],
  1,
  "The history names the cause: a toddler drinking large volumes of cow's milk, which contains very little bioavailable iron, and eating little else. The smear and indices confirm it — small, pale cells with a wide central pallor and a high red cell distribution width.\n\nThe lead-in asks for the MECHANISM of the small size, which the review file states in one line. An erythroid precursor divides while it accumulates hemoglobin and STOPS dividing once it reaches a target hemoglobin concentration. Without iron → it cannot reach that concentration on schedule → it undergoes an extra division or two before maturing → each division halves the cell. One mechanism, two findings: MICROCYTIC and HYPOCHROMIC.\n\nContrast the megaloblastic anemias, where the cytoplasm matures normally but the nucleus cannot divide, so the cell ends up too BIG. Same organ, opposite direction — which is why the mean corpuscular volume splits the differential so cleanly.",
  {"0": "Splenic removal of membrane produces SPHEROCYTES: the cell loses surface area, rounds up, loses its central pallor, and its hemoglobin concentration RISES. This smear shows the opposite — cells with an enlarged central pallor — and there is no splenomegaly or jaundice.",
   "2": "Impaired globin synthesis is the mechanism of thalassemia, which also gives small cells. It would be correct with target cells, a normal or high red cell count, a NORMAL red cell distribution width, and a family history — not in a toddler with an obvious dietary cause and a markedly wide distribution width.",
   "3": "Cation leak with red cell dehydration shrinks cells in conditions such as hereditary xerocytosis. It produces dense, hyperchromic cells with a raised hemoglobin concentration and signs of hemolysis, not pale cells with a low reticulocyte count.",
   "4": "Immature erythrocytes released early are reticulocytes, which are LARGER than mature cells, not smaller. A premature-release picture would come with a raised reticulocyte count; his is 0.8%."},
  "A young red cell keeps dividing until it has filled up with enough hemoglobin. Without iron it never fills up in time, so it divides a couple of extra times — and every split makes it smaller.",
  image="fig_ida_smear",
  imcap="Peripheral blood smear. Most erythrocytes are smaller than the nucleus of the lymphocyte near the center of the field and show an enlarged zone of central pallor; scattered cells are irregular in shape.")

# ── 3. Iron absorption — the duodenum (§4) ─────────────────────────────────
q("A 38-year-old woman comes to the office because of a 6-month history of fatigue. Three years ago she underwent Roux-en-Y gastric bypass for obesity. She has taken ferrous sulfate 325 mg by mouth daily for the past 6 months and reports taking every dose. Her menstrual periods are regular and light. Her only other medication is a daily multivitamin. Physical examination shows conjunctival pallor and well-healed laparoscopic scars.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 9.6 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 29% (N=36%–46%)\nMean corpuscular volume 74 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nRed cell distribution width 17.2% (N=11.5%–14.5%)\nSerum ferritin 6 ng/mL (N=10–120 ng/mL)\nTotal iron-binding capacity 438 µg/dL (N=250–400 µg/dL)\nC-reactive protein 0.3 mg/dL (N=<1.0 mg/dL)\nSerum vitamin B12 610 pg/mL (N=200–900 pg/mL)\n\nWhich of the following best explains the failure of oral iron therapy in this patient?",
  ["A. Bypass of the duodenum and proximal jejunum", "B. Decreased hepatic synthesis of transferrin", "C. Increased hepatic production of hepcidin", "D. Loss of intrinsic factor from gastric parietal cells", "E. Retention of absorbed iron within ileal enterocytes"],
  0,
  "Dietary iron is absorbed in the DUODENUM, and only as Fe²⁺, at roughly 1–2 mg per day. The review file lists what breaks that station: celiac disease, chronic diarrhea, gastrectomy and bariatric surgery, and acid suppression — anything that removes or bypasses the duodenum, or removes gastric acid.\n\nRoux-en-Y gastric bypass does both. Food passes from a small gastric pouch straight into the jejunum → the duodenum and proximal jejunum never see the iron → absorption fails no matter how faithfully the tablets are taken. The small pouch also produces little acid, so less Fe³⁺ is reduced to the absorbable Fe²⁺ form.\n\nThe panel rules out the alternatives. The pattern is pure iron deficiency — low ferritin, high total iron-binding capacity, high red cell distribution width. A normal C-reactive protein removes an inflammatory hepcidin block. Her menstrual losses are light, so there is no competing blood-loss explanation. Management is intravenous iron, which bypasses the gut entirely, with lifelong monitoring for both iron and B12 deficiency.",
  {"1": "Transferrin synthesis actually INCREASES in iron deficiency, which is exactly why her total iron-binding capacity is high at 438 µg/dL. Decreased transferrin synthesis — a low binding capacity — is the pattern of inflammation or liver disease.",
   "2": "Increased hepcidin blocks iron export through ferroportin and is the mechanism of anemia of chronic disease. It would be correct with a high ferritin, a LOW binding capacity, and an elevated C-reactive protein. Her ferritin is 6 ng/mL and her C-reactive protein is normal.",
   "3": "Loss of intrinsic factor is the right consequence of gastric surgery but the WRONG VITAMIN — it causes B12 deficiency and a macrocytic anemia. Her B12 of 610 pg/mL is normal and her cells are microcytic.",
   "4": "Iron is absorbed proximally, not in the ileum; the ileum is where the B12–intrinsic factor complex is taken up. Trapping of absorbed iron within enterocytes is a hepcidin effect, which her normal inflammatory markers argue against."},
  "Iron gets soaked up in the first part of the small intestine, right after the stomach. Her surgery rerouted food around that part, so the pills pass by the only place that could absorb them.")

# ── 4. Ferritin is an acute phase reactant (§4, §6) ────────────────────────
q("A 72-year-old man is admitted to the hospital because of a flare of rheumatoid arthritis with swelling of both wrists and knees. For the past year he has taken naproxen 500 mg twice daily. He reports two episodes of black stools in the past month.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 8.9 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\nHematocrit 27% (N=41%–53%)\nMean corpuscular volume 76 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nReticulocyte count 0.6% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\nSerum iron 22 µg/dL (N=65–175 µg/dL)\nTotal iron-binding capacity 310 µg/dL (N=250–400 µg/dL)\nSerum ferritin 95 ng/mL (N=20–250 ng/mL)\nTransferrin saturation 7% (N=20%–50%)\nC-reactive protein 6.8 mg/dL (N=<1.0 mg/dL)\n\nWhich of the following findings would most reliably establish that this patient has depleted iron stores?",
  ["A. Absence of stainable iron on bone marrow aspirate", "B. Decrease in the reticulocyte count on repeat testing", "C. Low serum iron concentration", "D. Normal serum ferritin concentration", "E. Normal total iron-binding capacity"],
  0,
  "Two processes are running at once, and the panel is deliberately ambiguous because of it. The black stools and daily naproxen make GASTROINTESTINAL BLOOD LOSS likely. The rheumatoid flare and a C-reactive protein of 6.8 mg/dL make INFLAMMATION certain.\n\nFERRITIN IS AN ACUTE PHASE REACTANT. It rises in inflammation, infection, liver disease and malignancy regardless of iron stores → a genuinely iron-deficient patient who is also inflamed can show a normal ferritin. His ferritin of 95 ng/mL therefore cannot be trusted to say his stores are adequate. The total iron-binding capacity is caught between the two processes as well: deficiency pushes it up, inflammation pulls it down, and it lands in the middle.\n\nThe review file's rule for exactly this mixed picture: when the pattern is mixed, the transferrin saturation and a MARROW IRON STAIN settle it. Absent stainable iron in marrow macrophages on Prussian blue is the most direct evidence that stores are empty. In pure anemia of chronic disease, macrophage iron is INCREASED, so the stain separates the two cleanly.",
  {"1": "The reticulocyte count is low in iron deficiency AND in anemia of chronic disease, because both are production defects. A falling count shows the marrow is under-producing but says nothing about why.",
   "2": "Serum iron is low in both conditions, so the review file states plainly that it discriminates nothing. It is the classic distractor in this comparison.",
   "3": "A normal ferritin is what this patient already has, and it is exactly the finding that CANNOT be trusted during active inflammation. Treating it as reassurance would miss a bleeding gastrointestinal lesion.",
   "4": "A normal total iron-binding capacity is the indeterminate result the two processes produce when they pull in opposite directions. Only a clearly HIGH value would argue for deficiency; normal leaves the question open."},
  "When the body is inflamed, the blood test that usually measures stored iron goes up even if the tank is empty. Looking directly at the bone marrow for iron is the way to know for sure.")

# ── 5. Iron studies — anemia of chronic disease (§6) ───────────────────────
q("A 44-year-old woman is evaluated for fatigue 5 months after she was diagnosed with nodular sclerosis Hodgkin lymphoma. She has not yet started treatment. She has night sweats and has lost 5 kg (11 lb). Her menstrual periods are regular and light, and she has no history of gastrointestinal bleeding.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 10.0 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 30% (N=36%–46%)\nMean corpuscular volume 83 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nReticulocyte count 0.5% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\nErythrocyte sedimentation rate 64 mm/h (N=0–20 mm/h)\n\nFurther studies of serum iron, serum ferritin, total iron-binding capacity, and marrow macrophage iron are planned, in that order.\n\nWhich of the following sets of findings is most likely?",
  ["A. Decreased, decreased, increased, absent", "B. Decreased, increased, decreased, increased", "C. Decreased, normal, normal, decreased", "D. Increased, increased, decreased, increased", "E. Normal, decreased, normal, decreased"],
  1,
  "A normocytic, hypoproliferative anemia developing months into a malignancy, with a high sedimentation rate and no source of blood loss, is ANEMIA OF CHRONIC DISEASE (anemia of inflammation). The review file lists its causes as chronic infections, chronic immune disorders, and neoplasms — Hodgkin disease by name — and notes it can appear as early as 1–2 months after disease onset.\n\nRead the row through the mechanism. Interleukin-6 → hepatic HEPCIDIN rises → ferroportin is destroyed → iron is locked inside macrophages and enterocytes.\n• Serum iron DECREASED — none is circulating.\n• Ferritin INCREASED — iron is stored, and ferritin is also an acute phase reactant.\n• Total iron-binding capacity DECREASED — transferrin is a negative acute phase reactant, so there are fewer cups on the shelf.\n• Marrow macrophage iron INCREASED — the iron is trapped exactly where hepcidin locked it.\n\nThe pair that separates this from iron deficiency is ferritin and total iron-binding capacity, which move in OPPOSITE directions in the two conditions.",
  {"0": "This is the iron deficiency row: empty tank (low ferritin), extra empty cups (high binding capacity), and no marrow iron. It would be correct if she had heavy menses or gastrointestinal bleeding and a microcytic anemia.",
   "2": "Low-normal ferritin, an indeterminate binding capacity and REDUCED marrow iron is the COMBINED picture — iron deficiency and inflammation together. It would fit if she also had a source of chronic blood loss, which the stem excludes.",
   "3": "High serum iron, high ferritin, low binding capacity and increased marrow iron describe iron overload — hereditary hemochromatosis, transfusional overload or a sideroblastic anemia. The HIGH serum iron is the discriminator; in anemia of chronic disease serum iron is low.",
   "4": "Falling ferritin with a still-normal serum iron and binding capacity is the EARLIEST stage of iron depletion, before anemia develops. It cannot explain an established anemia, and it would not arise from an inflammatory process."},
  "Inflammation makes the body hide its iron inside storage cells. The storage number looks high, but almost none of the iron is out in the blood where the marrow can use it.")

# ── 6. Hepcidin and ferroportin (§6) ───────────────────────────────────────
q("A 71-year-old woman with a 20-year history of rheumatoid arthritis comes to the office for follow-up of anemia. For the past 3 months she has taken ferrous sulfate 325 mg by mouth daily, with no change in her hemoglobin concentration. Her joint disease remains active, with synovitis of both wrists.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 9.6 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 29% (N=36%–46%)\nMean corpuscular volume 86 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nReticulocyte count 0.6% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\nSerum iron 31 µg/dL (N=50–170 µg/dL)\nSerum ferritin 340 ng/mL (N=10–120 ng/mL)\nTotal iron-binding capacity 212 µg/dL (N=250–400 µg/dL)\nC-reactive protein 4.1 mg/dL (N=<1.0 mg/dL)\n\nIncreased activity of which of the following best explains the lack of response to oral iron?",
  ["A. Divalent metal transporter 1", "B. Duodenal cytochrome b", "C. Ferroportin", "D. Hepcidin", "E. Transferrin receptor 1"],
  3,
  "High ferritin, low serum iron, low total iron-binding capacity, a normal MCV and active inflammation = ANEMIA OF CHRONIC DISEASE. The question is why the tablets did nothing.\n\nInflammation → interleukin-6 → the liver makes more HEPCIDIN → hepcidin binds FERROPORTIN, the only exit iron has from an enterocyte or a macrophage, and causes it to be internalized and destroyed → the doors are locked.\n\nThe review file's memory hook: hepcidin is the doorman who locks the exits. The iron is in the building; it cannot reach the marrow. That single image explains the whole picture — ferritin high because iron is stored, serum iron low because none is circulating — and the treatment failure: oral iron is absorbed into enterocytes and then cannot leave them, because the exit is gone. The management is to control the rheumatoid arthritis.",
  {"0": "Divalent metal transporter 1 carries Fe²⁺ from the gut lumen INTO the enterocyte. Increasing its activity would improve iron uptake, not block it — and in this patient the problem is iron LEAVING the cell, not entering it.",
   "1": "Duodenal cytochrome b is the ferrireductase that reduces dietary Fe³⁺ to absorbable Fe²⁺. Increased activity would enhance absorption; its loss, as with acid suppression, is what would impair it.",
   "2": "Ferroportin is the right molecule but the wrong direction — the intended trap. Increased ferroportin would EXPORT more iron into plasma and improve her response. In anemia of chronic disease ferroportin activity is DECREASED, because hepcidin destroys it.",
   "4": "Transferrin receptor 1 brings transferrin-bound iron into erythroid precursors. Its expression rises when cells are iron-hungry, and increased activity would support erythropoiesis rather than prevent iron from reaching the marrow."},
  "Inflammation tells the liver to make a hormone that locks the doors iron uses to leave the gut and the storage cells. Swallowing more iron just piles it up behind a locked door.",
  exim="fig_hepcidin",
  excap="Hepcidin, made in the liver, inhibits ferroportin-1 on enterocytes and macrophages — the only exits for iron into the blood.")

# ── 7. Methylmalonic acid separates B12 from folate (§5) ───────────────────
q("A 66-year-old woman comes to the office because of a 4-month history of fatigue. She lives alone and eats a limited diet of toast, tea, and canned soup. She does not drink alcoholic beverages and takes no medications. Neurologic examination shows no abnormalities; vibration sense and proprioception are intact.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 9.3 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 28% (N=36%–46%)\nMean corpuscular volume 114 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nLeukocyte count 3,900/mm3 (N=4,500–11,000/mm3)\nPlatelet count 142,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\nReticulocyte count 0.6% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\nSerum vitamin B12 238 pg/mL (N=200–900 pg/mL)\nSerum folate 3.1 ng/mL (N=2.5–20 ng/mL)\n\nA peripheral blood smear is shown.\n\nWhich of the following additional findings would most strongly indicate vitamin B12 deficiency as the cause of this patient's anemia?",
  ["A. Decreased serum haptoglobin concentration", "B. Increased red cell distribution width", "C. Increased serum homocysteine concentration", "D. Increased serum lactate dehydrogenase activity", "E. Increased serum methylmalonic acid concentration"],
  4,
  "Macro-ovalocytes and a hypersegmented neutrophil with pancytopenia mean defective DNA synthesis — B12 or folate. Both vitamin levels are at the bottom of their reference ranges, so the serum levels alone cannot decide which one is missing. A normal neurologic examination does not decide it either, because neurologic signs can lag behind the anemia.\n\nThe discriminator is the one metabolite only B12 controls. B12 has two jobs: it regenerates methionine from HOMOCYSTEINE (shared with folate), and it is the cofactor for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, which converts METHYLMALONYL-CoA to succinyl-CoA (B12 only).\n• Homocysteine rises in BOTH deficiencies → it only tells you that one of the two is missing.\n• Methylmalonic acid rises in B12 deficiency ONLY → it names the vitamin.\n\nReview file memory hook: MMA rises with B12 — the M's go together. If you can order one test to separate them, order the methylmalonic acid.",
  {"0": "A low haptoglobin reflects hemolysis, and megaloblastic anemias do lower it through intramedullary destruction of precursors. But this happens equally in B12 and folate deficiency, so it cannot discriminate between them.",
   "1": "A wide red cell distribution width shows anisocytosis. It is increased in both megaloblastic anemias, and in iron deficiency too, so it carries no information about which vitamin is missing.",
   "2": "Homocysteine is the tempting near-miss. It is elevated in B12 deficiency, but it is EQUALLY elevated in folate deficiency, because both vitamins are needed to convert homocysteine back to methionine. A raised value would be expected here whichever vitamin is deficient.",
   "3": "Lactate dehydrogenase rises from ineffective erythropoiesis in any megaloblastic anemia, and can be strikingly high. It confirms intramedullary cell death but is shared by both deficiencies."},
  "Two vitamins are needed for one step in making DNA, so a shortage of either gives the same big red cells. Only B12 is also needed to clear a substance called methylmalonic acid, so if that is high, B12 is the one missing.",
  image="fig_hyperseg_uw",
  imcap="Peripheral blood smear. Many erythrocytes are large and oval. The neutrophil in the center of the field has a nucleus with six lobes.")

# ── 8. Folate masks B12 deficiency (§5) ────────────────────────────────────
q("A 64-year-old man comes to the office because of a 6-week history of numbness in both feet and unsteadiness when walking, especially in the dark. Four months ago he was found to have a macrocytic anemia and was started on folic acid 1 mg daily by another physician; no further testing was performed. He feels less fatigued since starting treatment. Neurologic examination shows decreased vibration sense and proprioception in the toes and ankles, and a positive Romberg sign. Muscle strength and bulk are normal, with no fasciculations. Deep tendon reflexes are brisk at the knees, and plantar responses are extensor bilaterally. Pain and temperature sensation are intact.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 13.9 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\nHematocrit 42% (N=41%–53%)\nMean corpuscular volume 98 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nSerum vitamin B12 104 pg/mL (N=200–900 pg/mL)\nSerum folate 18.2 ng/mL (N=2.5–20 ng/mL)\n\nDegeneration of which of the following spinal cord structures is most likely responsible for this patient's findings?",
  ["A. Anterior horn cells", "B. Anterior white commissure", "C. Dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts", "D. Dorsal columns only", "E. Lateral spinothalamic tracts"],
  2,
  "This is the complication the review file warns about by name: NEVER GIVE FOLATE ALONE TO A MACROCYTIC ANEMIA.\n\nB12 has two jobs; folate has one. Both feed DNA synthesis, so folate supplementation corrects the anemia of B12 deficiency — his hemoglobin has normalized and his MCV has nearly normalized. But ONLY B12 maintains the myelin surrounding neurons. Folate has no effect on that second job → the blood count recovers, everyone relaxes → the neurologic degeneration continues unopposed toward irreversibility.\n\nThe lesion is SUBACUTE COMBINED DEGENERATION: myelin degeneration of the DORSAL COLUMNS and the LATERAL CORTICOSPINAL TRACTS.\n• Dorsal columns → loss of vibration and proprioception, sensory ataxia, positive Romberg sign.\n• Lateral corticospinal tracts → upper motor neuron signs: brisk reflexes and extensor plantar responses.\n\nThe rule the review file gives: always check a B12 level before treating a megaloblastic anemia with folate. Treatment now is parenteral B12, and the neurologic deficits may not fully recover.",
  {"0": "Anterior horn cell loss produces pure lower motor neuron findings — weakness, atrophy and fasciculations — with normal sensation, as in poliomyelitis. His strength and bulk are normal and his deficit is sensory.",
   "1": "The anterior white commissure is where crossing pain and temperature fibers are damaged in syringomyelia, giving a cape-like loss of pain and temperature. His pain and temperature sensation are explicitly intact.",
   "3": "Isolated dorsal column degeneration explains the lost vibration and proprioception and the Romberg sign, as in tabes dorsalis. It cannot explain the brisk knee reflexes and extensor plantar responses, which require corticospinal tract involvement.",
   "4": "The lateral spinothalamic tracts carry pain and temperature. Their involvement would produce contralateral loss of those modalities, which he does not have."},
  "Folic acid fixed his blood count but cannot protect nerves — only B12 does that. So the anemia got better while the insulation around his spinal cord nerves kept wearing away.")

# ── 9. Pernicious anemia — the gastric cancer risk (§5) ────────────────────
q("A 58-year-old woman with Hashimoto thyroiditis and vitiligo comes to the office because of a 5-month history of fatigue and a sore tongue. Her only medication is levothyroxine. Examination shows a smooth, beefy-red tongue and patchy depigmentation of the hands.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 8.8 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 26% (N=36%–46%)\nMean corpuscular volume 118 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nReticulocyte count 0.5% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\nSerum vitamin B12 92 pg/mL (N=200–900 pg/mL)\nLactate dehydrogenase 780 U/L (N=45–200 U/L)\n\nSerum anti-intrinsic factor antibodies are present. Upper endoscopy shows thin mucosa with loss of rugal folds in the gastric body and fundus; the antrum appears normal. She is treated with intramuscular vitamin B12, and her hemoglobin concentration returns to normal over 8 weeks.\n\nDespite normalization of her hemoglobin, this patient requires ongoing surveillance because she is at increased risk for which of the following?",
  ["A. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia", "B. Colorectal adenocarcinoma", "C. Gastric adenocarcinoma", "D. Hepatocellular carcinoma", "E. Pancreatic adenocarcinoma"],
  2,
  "PERNICIOUS ANEMIA is an autoimmune disease, not a diet problem. Autoimmune destruction of the gastric mucosa → loss of parietal cells in the BODY and FUNDUS → no intrinsic factor → no B12 absorption in the terminal ileum, however much B12 she eats. The associated autoimmune diseases (Hashimoto thyroiditis, vitiligo), the anti-intrinsic factor antibody, and atrophy sparing the antrum all point the same way.\n\nThe review file frames the lesson as THREE ORGANS, NOT ONE:\n1. Megaloblastic anemia — from the B12 deficiency.\n2. Gastric atrophy with intestinal metaplasia — from the immune-mediated destruction → an increased risk of GASTRIC CARCINOMA.\n3. Central nervous system lesions — myelin degeneration of the dorsal and lateral columns.\n\nIntramuscular B12 fixes problem 1 and halts problem 3. It does nothing for problem 2 — the atrophic, metaplastic stomach remains, and with it the cancer risk. That is why these patients need ongoing surveillance after the blood count normalizes. Treating pernicious anemia means treating three problems.",
  {"0": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia is associated with autoimmune HEMOLYTIC anemia and immune thrombocytopenia, not with autoimmune gastritis. It would be suspected with a lymphocytosis and smudge cells, which are not described.",
   "1": "Colorectal adenocarcinoma is the surface-read answer: anemia in an adult prompts a search for a gastrointestinal cancer. That rule applies to IRON deficiency from blood loss. Her anemia is macrocytic from B12 malabsorption, and the organ at risk is the atrophic stomach, not the colon.",
   "3": "Hepatocellular carcinoma arises in cirrhosis from hepatitis B or C, alcohol or hemochromatosis. Nothing in her history or endoscopy points to chronic liver disease.",
   "4": "Pancreatic adenocarcinoma is not linked to autoimmune gastritis. Pancreatic insufficiency can impair B12 absorption by failing to free B12 from haptocorrin, but that is a cause of deficiency, not a consequence of pernicious anemia."},
  "Her immune system attacked the part of her stomach that helps absorb B12. The shots fix the vitamin, but the damaged stomach lining stays damaged and has a higher chance of turning into cancer, so doctors keep checking it.")

# ── 10. Ineffective erythropoiesis (§5) ────────────────────────────────────
q("A 61-year-old man comes to the office because of a 3-month history of fatigue, mild confusion, and tingling in his fingers. His temperature is 36.8°C (98.2°F), pulse is 102/min, and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. Examination shows pallor and mild scleral icterus. There is no splenomegaly.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 7.4 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\nHematocrit 22% (N=41%–53%)\nMean corpuscular volume 121 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nLeukocyte count 3,600/mm3 (N=4,500–11,000/mm3)\nPlatelet count 118,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\nReticulocyte count 0.4% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\nLactate dehydrogenase 1,450 U/L (N=45–200 U/L)\nHaptoglobin 22 mg/dL (N=30–200 mg/dL)\nTotal bilirubin 2.1 mg/dL (N=0.1–1.0 mg/dL)\nDirect bilirubin 0.3 mg/dL (N=0.0–0.3 mg/dL)\nSerum vitamin B12 110 pg/mL (N=200–900 pg/mL)\n\nA bone marrow aspirate is obtained; the marrow is hypercellular. A representative field is shown.\n\nWhich of the following best explains the combination of a hypercellular marrow and a low reticulocyte count?",
  ["A. Destruction of erythroid precursors within the marrow before release", "B. Failure of renal erythropoietin production", "C. Immune-mediated destruction of circulating erythrocytes in the spleen", "D. Replacement of hematopoietic tissue by marrow fibrosis", "E. Suppression of hematopoietic stem cells by cytotoxic T lymphocytes"],
  0,
  "The marrow is BUSY and the blood is EMPTY. That contradiction has one name: INEFFECTIVE ERYTHROPOIESIS.\n\nThe review file's single idea behind every megaloblastic finding is NUCLEAR–CYTOPLASMIC ASYNCHRONY. Hemoglobin synthesis in the cytoplasm proceeds normally, because it needs iron, not DNA. Nuclear maturation and cell division stall, because they need thymidine → which needs folate → which needs B12 to be regenerated. The cytoplasm outruns the nucleus. The aspirate shows the result: large precursors with fine, open chromatin.\n\nMany of those precursors cannot complete division and DIE INSIDE THE MARROW before release → hypercellular marrow (many cells produced) + low reticulocyte count (few released) + raised LDH and indirect bilirubin, with a lowered haptoglobin, from intramedullary cell death.\n\nThis is the trap: the hemolysis panel moves as a unit here, and a hurried reader calls it peripheral hemolysis. The LOW reticulocyte count is the buried clue that kills that reading. Peripheral destruction with an intact marrow drives the reticulocyte count UP; destruction inside the marrow does not.",
  {"1": "Erythropoietin failure, as in chronic kidney disease, produces a HYPOCELLULAR or normocellular marrow with a normocytic anemia. Lack of the hormone means too few precursors are made — the opposite of this packed marrow.",
   "2": "Immune destruction in the spleen is extravascular hemolysis. With an intact marrow it drives a brisk RETICULOCYTOSIS and splenomegaly. His reticulocyte count is 0.4% and he has no splenomegaly, so the cells are dying before they ever reach the circulation.",
   "3": "Marrow fibrosis gives a dry tap, teardrop cells, a leukoerythroblastic smear and massive splenomegaly. The marrow was aspirated successfully and is hypercellular, not replaced.",
   "4": "T-cell suppression of stem cells is the mechanism of aplastic anemia, which gives a HYPOCELLULAR, fatty marrow with a normal-to-slightly-raised MCV and no hemolysis markers — the reverse of every marrow finding here."},
  "His marrow is making plenty of young red cells, but they cannot finish dividing because they lack B12, so most of them die before they leave. The dying cells release chemicals into the blood that look like red cells breaking apart.",
  image="fig_megaloblastic_marrow",
  imcap="Bone marrow aspirate (high power). The field is highly cellular. Many precursor cells are enlarged, with nuclei showing finely dispersed, open chromatin, and several granulocytic cells are larger than expected.")

# ── 11. Metformin and B12 (§5) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
q("A 67-year-old man with a 14-year history of type 2 diabetes mellitus comes to the office for routine follow-up. He reports increasing fatigue over the past year and numbness of both feet. His medications are amlodipine, aspirin, atorvastatin, lisinopril, and metformin 1,000 mg twice daily. He eats a diet that includes meat and dairy. He does not drink alcoholic beverages and has had no gastrointestinal surgery.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 11.1 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\nHematocrit 33% (N=41%–53%)\nMean corpuscular volume 106 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nHemoglobin A1c 7.1% (N=≤6%)\nSerum vitamin B12 162 pg/mL (N=200–900 pg/mL)\nSerum folate 11.4 ng/mL (N=2.5–20 ng/mL)\nMethylmalonic acid 0.92 µmol/L (N=0.08–0.40 µmol/L)\n\nA peripheral blood smear shows oval macrocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils.\n\nWhich of the following medications is the most likely cause of this patient's anemia?",
  ["A. Amlodipine", "B. Aspirin", "C. Atorvastatin", "D. Lisinopril", "E. Metformin"],
  4,
  "Macrocytosis, hypersegmented neutrophils, a low B12 and a raised METHYLMALONIC ACID — with a normal folate — confirm VITAMIN B12 DEFICIENCY. He is not vegan, does not drink, and has no gastric or ileal surgery, so the review file's table of causes points to the drug-induced row.\n\nMETFORMIN interferes with CALCIUM-DEPENDENT uptake of the B12–intrinsic factor complex at the terminal ileum. Long-term, high-dose use — 14 years and 2 g daily here — produces a gradual deficiency.\n\nThe dramatic decoy is the numbness of his feet. In a man with long-standing diabetes it is easy to call that diabetic peripheral neuropathy and stop thinking. B12 deficiency ALSO causes a neuropathy and dorsal column loss, and the two can coexist. Patients on long-term metformin should have their B12 checked, particularly when neuropathy or anemia appears. The other drug on the review file's list is the proton pump inhibitor — without acid, B12 is never released from food protein.",
  {"0": "Amlodipine causes peripheral edema and gingival hyperplasia. It has no effect on B12 absorption or DNA synthesis.",
   "1": "Aspirin causes gastrointestinal blood loss, which leads to IRON deficiency — a microcytic anemia with a low ferritin. His MCV is 106 µm3 with a raised methylmalonic acid, which is the opposite picture.",
   "2": "Atorvastatin is associated with myopathy and elevated liver enzymes. It does not impair B12 absorption and would not produce hypersegmented neutrophils.",
   "3": "Lisinopril can cause hyperkalemia, cough and angioedema, and angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors can modestly lower hemoglobin by reducing erythropoietin. That anemia is normocytic, without hypersegmented neutrophils or a raised methylmalonic acid."},
  "His diabetes pill slowly blocks the gut from soaking up vitamin B12. After years of it, he ran low on B12 — which made his red cells big and can also numb his feet.")

# ── 12. Folic acid before conception (§5) ──────────────────────────────────
q("A 27-year-old woman comes to the office for a health maintenance examination. She plans to stop her oral contraceptive next month in order to conceive. She has never been pregnant. She has no history of serious illness, and there is no family history of congenital anomalies. She eats a balanced diet that includes leafy green vegetables. Vital signs are within normal limits, and physical examination shows no abnormalities.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding folic acid?",
  ["A. Begin folic acid 0.4 mg daily now, before attempting conception", "B. Begin folic acid 4 mg daily after the first positive pregnancy test", "C. Begin folic acid after an ultrasound confirms a viable intrauterine pregnancy", "D. Folic acid supplementation is unnecessary with a diet rich in green vegetables", "E. Measure serum folate and supplement only if the concentration is low"],
  0,
  "FOLATE IS THE KEY NUTRIENT FOR NEURAL TUBE CLOSURE. Supplementation prevents the neural tube defects spina bifida and anencephaly.\n\nThe timing is the whole point, and the review file states it directly. The neural tube closes at about 4 WEEKS after conception → often before a pregnancy is even recognized → by the time a pregnancy test turns positive, the window may already be closing. The recommendation is therefore to supplement BEFORE CONCEPTION, in all women of childbearing potential, not after a positive test.\n\nThe standard dose for a woman with no prior affected pregnancy is 0.4 mg daily. Pregnancy is also a state of increased folate requirement in its own right, which is why every prenatal vitamin contains more folic acid than a general multivitamin.",
  {"1": "Four milligrams daily is the higher dose reserved for women who have had a PREVIOUS pregnancy affected by a neural tube defect. Starting after a positive test is also too late, because closure is complete around 4 weeks — often before a test would be positive.",
   "2": "Waiting for an ultrasound delays supplementation until well after the neural tube has already closed. It would miss the entire protective window.",
   "3": "Green vegetables are the richest dietary source of folate, which is why this distractor is tempting. Diet alone does not reliably supply the amount needed around conception, and the recommendation applies regardless of diet.",
   "4": "A serum folate level reflects recent intake and does not predict neural tube defect risk. Testing before supplementing adds delay and cost to an intervention that is cheap, safe and recommended for everyone planning pregnancy."},
  "The baby's spinal cord forms in the first few weeks, before most people even know they are pregnant. Taking folic acid ahead of time makes sure it is already there when that happens.")

# ── 13. Terminal ileum — where B12 is absorbed (§5) ────────────────────────
q("A 34-year-old man with Crohn disease comes to the office because of a 3-month history of fatigue and tingling in his feet. Four years ago he underwent resection of 90 cm of terminal ileum for a stricture. He eats meat and dairy. He takes no medications and does not drink alcoholic beverages.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 10.2 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\nHematocrit 31% (N=41%–53%)\nMean corpuscular volume 109 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nSerum vitamin B12 128 pg/mL (N=200–900 pg/mL)\nSerum folate 14.0 ng/mL (N=2.5–20 ng/mL)\nMethylmalonic acid 0.88 µmol/L (N=0.08–0.40 µmol/L)\n\nSerum anti-intrinsic factor and anti-parietal cell antibodies are absent.\n\nAbsorption of vitamin B12 is impaired in this patient because of loss of which of the following?",
  ["A. Cubilin receptors on terminal ileal enterocytes", "B. Haptocorrin secreted in saliva", "C. Intrinsic factor secreted by gastric parietal cells", "D. Pancreatic proteases acting in the duodenum", "E. Transcobalamin II in the plasma"],
  0,
  "The review file describes B12 metabolism as a long road with many exits, and every station is a place a deficiency can start:\n1. Stomach — acid and pepsin liberate B12 from food protein; salivary haptocorrin carries it onward.\n2. Stomach — parietal cells secrete intrinsic factor.\n3. Duodenum — pancreatic proteases split haptocorrin, freeing B12 to bind intrinsic factor.\n4. TERMINAL ILEUM — the B12–intrinsic factor complex is absorbed via the CUBILIN receptor. This is the ONLY place it happens.\n5. Blood — transcobalamin II delivers B12 to cells.\n\nResecting 90 cm of terminal ileum removes station 4. His stomach, pancreas and saliva are intact, and the absent antibodies exclude pernicious anemia — so intrinsic factor is being made and binding normally, but there is nowhere left to absorb the complex. The macrocytosis, low B12 and raised methylmalonic acid confirm the deficiency.\n\nThe historical Schilling test localized exactly this lesion: radiolabeled B12 is not absorbed in stage I, and adding intrinsic factor in stage II STILL does not correct it → the defect is at the terminal ileum, not the stomach.",
  {"1": "Haptocorrin carries B12 through the stomach, but its loss is not a recognized cause of deficiency, and his salivary glands are unaffected by ileal surgery.",
   "2": "Loss of intrinsic factor is pernicious anemia or gastric surgery. It would be correct with anti-intrinsic factor or anti-parietal cell antibodies, or a gastrectomy. His antibodies are absent and his stomach is intact.",
   "3": "Pancreatic insufficiency does impair B12 absorption by failing to free B12 from haptocorrin, and would be correct in chronic pancreatitis or cystic fibrosis. His pancreas is untouched by the resection.",
   "4": "Transcobalamin II carries B12 in the plasma AFTER it has been absorbed. A congenital deficiency presents in infancy, and loss of it would not follow bowel resection."},
  "Vitamin B12 can only get into the body at one specific spot at the end of the small intestine. That spot was removed in his surgery, so the vitamin passes straight through.",
  exim="fig_b12_absorption",
  excap="Vitamin B12 absorption: release from food protein in the stomach, transfer from haptocorrin to intrinsic factor in the duodenum, uptake via cubilin in the ileum, and transport on transcobalamin II.")

# ── 14. The hypersegmented neutrophil (§5) ─────────────────────────────────
q("A 52-year-old man is evaluated for fatigue. He drinks six to eight cans of beer daily and eats one meal a day, consisting mostly of fast food. He takes no medications. Physical examination shows no jaundice, ascites, or spider angiomas; the liver edge is not palpable.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 10.6 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\nHematocrit 32% (N=41%–53%)\nMean corpuscular volume 112 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nLeukocyte count 4,100/mm3 (N=4,500–11,000/mm3)\nPlatelet count 131,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\nAspartate aminotransferase 64 U/L (N=12–38 U/L)\nAlanine aminotransferase 38 U/L (N=10–40 U/L)\n\nA peripheral blood smear is shown.\n\nThe neutrophil morphology shown is most specifically caused by which of the following?",
  ["A. Accelerated release of immature neutrophils from the marrow storage pool", "B. Excess membrane lipid deposition from altered hepatic cholesterol metabolism", "C. Impaired thymidine synthesis delaying nuclear maturation", "D. Inherited failure of normal nuclear lobation", "E. Toxic granulation from cytokine-stimulated granulopoiesis"],
  2,
  "Macrocytosis has a long differential — alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism, drugs, reticulocytosis — and this man could fit several. The smear narrows it.\n\nA normal neutrophil has three to five lobes. SIX OR MORE is a HYPERSEGMENTED neutrophil, and the review file calls it the giveaway: it says the problem is specifically DNA SYNTHESIS, which narrows you immediately to B12 or folate. In this man, heavy alcohol use and a poor diet make FOLATE deficiency the likely cause.\n\nThe mechanism is nuclear–cytoplasmic asynchrony. DNA synthesis needs thymidine → thymidine synthesis needs folate (and B12 to regenerate it) → without it, nuclear maturation stalls in every dividing cell → granulocyte precursors enlarge and their nuclei become abnormally segmented as they mature. The same defect produces the macro-ovalocytes and giant bands.\n\nThe decoy is the alcohol and the raised aspartate aminotransferase, which surface-read toward the macrocytosis of liver disease. That macrocytosis comes from lipid added to the red cell membrane, produces round macrocytes and target cells, and never segments a neutrophil's nucleus.",
  {"0": "Accelerated release from the storage pool is a LEFT SHIFT — band forms and metamyelocytes with FEWER nuclear segments, as in bacterial infection. It moves the lobe count in the opposite direction.",
   "1": "Membrane lipid deposition is the mechanism of the round macrocytes and target cells of liver disease and alcohol use. It is the surface-read answer here, but it affects the red cell membrane and cannot change a neutrophil's nucleus.",
   "3": "Inherited failure of lobation is the Pelger–Huët anomaly, in which neutrophils are HYPOsegmented — typically bilobed, 'pince-nez' nuclei. It is the mirror image of what is shown.",
   "4": "Toxic granulation reflects cytokine-driven granulopoiesis in severe infection and inflammation, with coarse dark cytoplasmic granules and often Döhle bodies. It changes the cytoplasm, not the number of nuclear lobes."},
  "This white cell's nucleus is split into too many pieces. That happens when a cell cannot build DNA properly, which is what low folate or B12 does — and heavy drinkers with poor diets often run out of folate.",
  image="fig_hyperseg",
  imcap="Peripheral blood smear (very high power). A large neutrophil with a nucleus divided into six lobes is surrounded by erythrocytes, several of which are oval.")

# ── 15. Anemia of chronic disease — treat the cause (§6) ───────────────────
q("A 61-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus comes to the office because of fatigue. For 5 months he has had chronic osteomyelitis of the right calcaneus with a draining sinus tract, treated intermittently with oral antibiotics. He has no gastrointestinal symptoms. His temperature is 37.6°C (99.7°F), pulse is 88/min, and blood pressure is 132/80 mm Hg. Examination shows a 1-cm draining sinus over the right heel with surrounding erythema. Test of the stool for occult blood is negative.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 10.3 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\nHematocrit 31% (N=41%–53%)\nMean corpuscular volume 87 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nReticulocyte count 0.7% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\nSerum iron 34 µg/dL (N=65–175 µg/dL)\nSerum ferritin 410 ng/mL (N=20–250 ng/mL)\nTotal iron-binding capacity 218 µg/dL (N=250–400 µg/dL)\nErythrocyte sedimentation rate 88 mm/h (N=0–15 mm/h)\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate management of this patient's anemia?",
  ["A. Erythropoiesis-stimulating agent therapy", "B. Intravenous iron sucrose", "C. Oral ferrous sulfate", "D. Surgical debridement with culture-directed antibiotic therapy", "E. Transfusion of packed red blood cells"],
  3,
  "A normocytic, hypoproliferative anemia with LOW serum iron, HIGH ferritin, LOW total iron-binding capacity and a sedimentation rate of 88 mm/h, in a patient with a 5-month chronic infection, is ANEMIA OF CHRONIC DISEASE. The review file lists chronic infections — osteomyelitis first among them — as a classic cause. It is the most common cause of anemia among hospitalized patients in the United States, and a diagnosis of exclusion.\n\nThe management rule is stated plainly because the instinct to 'treat the number' is so strong: TREAT THE UNDERLYING DISEASE. The anemia is driven by the inflammatory cytokine response, not by a shortage of iron → interleukin-6 raises hepcidin → iron is locked away → erythropoietin release and erythroid proliferation are blunted. Remove the stimulus and all four mechanisms switch off.\n\nFor chronic osteomyelitis with a draining sinus, that means surgical debridement of infected bone and antibiotics directed by bone culture. As the infection resolves, the hemoglobin recovers on its own.",
  {"0": "Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents may be considered for symptomatic anemia of chronic disease, but the review file notes they are NOT FDA-approved for this indication and carry real safety concerns. They would be a second-line consideration after the infection is treated, not the primary management.",
   "1": "Intravenous iron bypasses the gut and is the right choice for a patient with true iron deficiency who cannot absorb oral iron. This man's ferritin of 410 ng/mL shows his stores are replete; the problem is iron release, not supply.",
   "2": "Oral iron generally does not help in anemia of chronic disease, because the enterocyte cannot export absorbed iron past a hepcidin-destroyed ferroportin. It would be correct only with a coexisting true iron deficiency, which his high ferritin and low binding capacity exclude.",
   "4": "Transfusion is reserved for severe or symptomatic anemia with hemodynamic compromise, active bleeding, or a much lower hemoglobin. He is stable at 10.3 g/dL with a correctable cause, so transfusion would add risk without addressing the problem."},
  "His bone infection is keeping his body in fight mode, and fight mode switches off red cell making. Clear the infection and the blood count comes back by itself.")

# ═════════════════════════════ PART 2 — Q16–Q30 ═════════════════════════════

# ── 16. Pica, menorrhagia, and how long to treat (§4) ─────────────────────
q("A 25-year-old woman comes to the office because of a 3-month history of fatigue and shortness of breath when walking uphill. She reports an intense craving to chew ice. Her menstrual periods last 8 days with passage of clots. She has followed a vegetarian diet for 4 years. She has no gastrointestinal symptoms. Physical examination shows conjunctival pallor; the abdomen is nontender.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 7.9 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 24% (N=36%–46%)\nMean corpuscular volume 71 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nRed cell distribution width 18.2% (N=11.5%–14.5%)\nPlatelet count 452,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\nSerum ferritin 4 ng/mL (N=10–120 ng/mL)\nTotal iron-binding capacity 470 µg/dL (N=250–400 µg/dL)\nTransferrin saturation 5% (N=20%–50%)\n\nOral ferrous sulfate is prescribed, and she is referred for gynecologic evaluation of her menstrual bleeding.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate plan for the duration of oral iron therapy?",
  ["A. Continue for approximately 3 months after the hemoglobin concentration normalizes", "B. Discontinue once the hemoglobin concentration normalizes", "C. Discontinue once the ice craving resolves", "D. Discontinue once the reticulocyte count peaks", "E. Replace with dietary modification once the hemoglobin concentration reaches 10 g/dL"],
  0,
  "This is iron deficiency anemia from two mechanisms at once, exactly as the review file's case describes: chronic loss through MENORRHAGIA plus reduced intake of bioavailable iron on a VEGETARIAN diet. The craving to chew ice is PICA — specifically pagophagia — which is distinctive and highly suggestive of iron deficiency. The ferritin of 4 ng/mL settles the diagnosis.\n\nThe lead-in is about DURATION, and the principle is that there are two pools to refill, in order:\n1. Functional iron — hemoglobin in circulating erythrocytes. This recovers first, over roughly 6–8 weeks.\n2. Storage iron — ferritin and hemosiderin in liver and macrophages. This was the FIRST pool to empty and is the LAST to refill.\n\nStopping when the hemoglobin normalizes leaves the stores empty → the next heavy period tips her straight back into anemia. So iron is continued for about 3 months after the hemoglobin normalizes to replete stores. The review file adds the other half of management: diet alone will not correct an established deficiency, and the cause of the loss must be addressed.\n\nNote the discrimination the review file makes: in a premenopausal woman with an obvious gynecologic source, a gastrointestinal workup is NOT automatic. The adult-male and postmenopausal rule does not apply here.",
  {"1": "Stopping at a normal hemoglobin is the most common error in practice. The hemoglobin recovers before the storage pool does, so the stores are still empty at that point and the anemia recurs with ongoing menstrual losses.",
   "2": "Pica often resolves within days to weeks of starting iron — well before the hemoglobin has recovered, let alone the stores. It is a useful early sign of response, not an endpoint for therapy.",
   "3": "The reticulocyte count peaks at about 7–10 days, which confirms that the diagnosis is right and the patient is absorbing the iron. Stopping then would leave both the hemoglobin and the stores uncorrected.",
   "4": "The review file states directly that diet alone will not correct an established deficiency. A vegetarian diet also supplies mostly nonheme iron, which is less well absorbed than heme iron."},
  "Her body uses iron first to rebuild the blood, and only after that to refill its storage tank. If she stops when her blood count looks normal, the tank is still empty and the anemia comes back.",
  exim="fig_ida_smear_uw",
  excap="Iron deficiency anemia: microcytic, hypochromic erythrocytes with enlarged central pallor.")

# ── 17. Black stools on oral iron (§4) ────────────────────────────────────
q("A 29-year-old woman calls the office 2 weeks after starting ferrous sulfate 325 mg daily for iron deficiency anemia caused by heavy menstrual bleeding. She reports that her stools have turned very dark and that she has mild constipation. She has no abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, lightheadedness, or hematemesis. She takes no other medications or over-the-counter products. She is seen the same day. Her pulse is 74/min and blood pressure is 116/72 mm Hg, without orthostatic change. The abdomen is soft and nontender. Rectal examination shows formed, dark green-black stool that is not tarry. Two weeks ago her hemoglobin concentration was 8.6 g/dL and her reticulocyte count was 0.8%.\n\nLaboratory studies today show:\nHemoglobin 9.4 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 28% (N=36%–46%)\nReticulocyte count 4.8% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely explanation for the change in her stool color?",
  ["A. Bismuth subsalicylate ingestion", "B. Bleeding duodenal ulcer", "C. Esophageal variceal hemorrhage", "D. Iron-induced stool discoloration", "E. Right-sided colon carcinoma"],
  3,
  "Unabsorbed oral iron is excreted in the stool and turns it DARK GREEN-BLACK. The review file lists black stools among the expected adverse effects of oral iron — alongside nausea, constipation and a metallic taste — and names them as the main reason patients stop taking it. Warning her up front is part of prescribing it.\n\nThe buried clues separate this from melena:\n• The stool is FORMED and NOT TARRY. Melena from upper gastrointestinal bleeding is sticky, tarry and foul-smelling, because hemoglobin is digested.\n• She is HEMODYNAMICALLY STABLE with no orthostatic change.\n• Most decisively, she is RESPONDING. Her reticulocyte count has risen from 0.8% to 4.8% and her hemoglobin from 8.6 to 9.4 g/dL. The review file says to expect a reticulocyte rise within about a week if treatment is working, and a patient who was actively bleeding enough to blacken her stool would not be gaining hemoglobin.\n\nManagement is reassurance and continuing the iron. Constipation is managed symptomatically, or by taking the dose with food at the cost of some absorption.",
  {"0": "Bismuth subsalicylate also turns stool black and is a classic cause of 'pseudomelena'. It would be correct if she were taking an over-the-counter antidiarrheal, but the stem states she takes no other medications or over-the-counter products.",
   "1": "A bleeding duodenal ulcer causes true melena — tarry, sticky stool — usually with epigastric pain, NSAID use, or hemodynamic change. A falling, not rising, hemoglobin would be expected.",
   "2": "Variceal hemorrhage is an upper gastrointestinal emergency in cirrhosis, with hematemesis, melena and hemodynamic instability. Nothing in her history suggests liver disease, and she is stable with a rising hemoglobin.",
   "4": "Right-sided colon carcinoma causes chronic occult blood loss and iron deficiency, but in older adults and generally without visible black stool. Her anemia already has a gynecologic explanation, and her rapid response to iron argues against ongoing loss."},
  "Iron pills that do not get absorbed come out in the stool and turn it very dark. Her blood count is already going up, which shows the iron is working and she is not bleeding.")

# ── 18. The first thing to fall — storage iron (§4) ───────────────────────
q("A 34-year-old man comes to a blood donation center for his scheduled donation. He has donated a unit of whole blood every 8 weeks for the past 3 years. He feels well and has no symptoms. He eats a varied diet that includes red meat. Physical examination shows no abnormalities.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 14.6 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\nHematocrit 44% (N=41%–53%)\nMean corpuscular volume 88 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nRed cell distribution width 13.1% (N=11.5%–14.5%)\nSerum iron 82 µg/dL (N=65–175 µg/dL)\nTotal iron-binding capacity 372 µg/dL (N=250–400 µg/dL)\nTransferrin saturation 22% (N=20%–50%)\nSerum ferritin 9 ng/mL (N=20–250 ng/mL)\n\nWhich of the following best describes this patient's iron status?",
  ["A. Anemia of chronic disease", "B. Depleted iron stores without iron-deficient erythropoiesis", "C. Established iron deficiency anemia", "D. Iron-deficient erythropoiesis without anemia", "E. Normal iron status"],
  1,
  "Every value here is normal EXCEPT the ferritin. That pattern describes the first stage of iron deficiency.\n\nThe review file's map of where iron sits explains why ferritin moves first. About 80% of body iron is FUNCTIONAL — mostly hemoglobin in circulating erythrocytes. About 20% is STORAGE — ferritin and hemosiderin in liver and macrophages, roughly 1,000 mg in a man. When iron is lost faster than it is absorbed, the body spends STORES first to protect hemoglobin. So deficiency progresses in a fixed order:\n1. DEPLETED STORES — ferritin falls; serum iron, transferrin saturation, MCV and hemoglobin all still normal. ← this donor\n2. Iron-deficient erythropoiesis — serum iron and transferrin saturation fall too; hemoglobin still normal.\n3. Iron deficiency anemia — MCV and hemoglobin finally fall.\n\nThe review file's memory hook: ferritin is the fuel gauge, the earliest thing to fall, before the hemoglobin ever moves; transferrin saturation is the fuel line. His gauge reads empty while fuel still reaches the engine. Each unit of whole blood removes roughly 200–250 mg of iron, so donation every 8 weeks outpaces absorption of 1–2 mg per day. He should defer donation or take iron supplements.",
  {"0": "Anemia of chronic disease requires anemia and inflammation, and it RAISES ferritin. This man has a normal hemoglobin, no illness, and a low ferritin — the opposite pattern.",
   "2": "Established iron deficiency anemia requires a low hemoglobin and microcytosis. His hemoglobin of 14.6 g/dL and MCV of 88 µm3 are normal — that stage is still ahead of him if donation continues.",
   "3": "Iron-deficient erythropoiesis is the next stage, marked by a falling serum iron and a transferrin saturation below normal. His transferrin saturation is 22%, still within range, so the fuel line is not yet affected.",
   "4": "A normal hemoglobin is the surface-read that makes this look normal. His ferritin of 9 ng/mL is below the reference range, and ferritin is the earliest and most sensitive marker of iron stores."},
  "The body keeps a reserve tank of iron and uses it up before letting the blood count drop. His blood count is fine, but his reserve tank is almost empty from giving blood so often.",
  exim="fig_ida_progression",
  excap="Laboratory changes with progressive iron deficiency: decreased storage first (ferritin alone), then iron-limited erythropoiesis, then anemia.")

# ── 19. Refractory iron deficiency — look for malabsorption (§4) ──────────
q("A 26-year-old woman comes to the office for follow-up of iron deficiency anemia. She has taken ferrous sulfate 325 mg by mouth daily as directed for 4 months, with no improvement. Her menstrual periods are regular and light. For 2 years she has had intermittent bloating and loose stools. She has an itchy rash on her elbows and knees. She eats a diet that includes meat and has not traveled outside the United States. Physical examination shows clustered, excoriated papules and vesicles on the extensor surfaces of both elbows and knees. A test of the stool for occult blood is negative.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 9.8 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 30% (N=36%–46%)\nMean corpuscular volume 73 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nSerum ferritin 5 ng/mL (N=10–120 ng/mL)\nTotal iron-binding capacity 448 µg/dL (N=250–400 µg/dL)\nSerum folate 2.2 ng/mL (N=2.5–20 ng/mL)\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate next step in diagnosis?",
  ["A. Colonoscopy", "B. Hemoglobin electrophoresis", "C. Serum anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA and total IgA concentrations", "D. Serum lead concentration", "E. Stool examination for ova and parasites"],
  2,
  "Iron deficiency that FAILS TO RESPOND to adequate oral iron in a patient who takes it faithfully means the iron is not getting in. The review file lists the causes of impaired absorption: celiac disease, chronic diarrhea, gastrectomy and bariatric surgery, and acid suppression — anything that removes or damages the DUODENUM.\n\nThis patient has the full picture of CELIAC DISEASE:\n• Chronic bloating and loose stools.\n• An itchy vesicular rash on the extensor surfaces — dermatitis herpetiformis.\n• Iron deficiency refractory to oral iron — the duodenum, where iron is absorbed as Fe²⁺, is the most affected segment.\n• A LOW FOLATE as well — folate is also absorbed proximally, so a combined iron-and-folate deficiency points at the proximal small bowel.\n\nThe review file's rule is that iron deficiency is a symptom, not a final diagnosis — always ask where the iron went. Here the iron was never absorbed. The first test is serologic: anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA, with a total IgA level, because selective IgA deficiency is more common in celiac disease and would give a false-negative result. A positive test is confirmed by duodenal biopsy showing villous atrophy.",
  {"0": "Colonoscopy is the right next step for iron deficiency in an adult man or a postmenopausal woman, where gastrointestinal blood loss must be excluded. She is 26, her stool is negative for occult blood, and her history points to malabsorption rather than bleeding.",
   "1": "Hemoglobin electrophoresis is for microcytosis with NORMAL iron studies — the thalassemia trait picture. Her ferritin of 5 ng/mL proves true iron deficiency.",
   "3": "Lead poisoning causes microcytic anemia with basophilic stippling and an exposure history, and does not lower ferritin or cause a vesicular rash. It would be correct in a child with pica for paint chips or a worker with occupational exposure.",
   "4": "Hookworm infection is a major cause of iron deficiency worldwide through intestinal blood loss. She has not traveled, her stool is negative for occult blood, and hookworm would not explain the rash or the low folate."},
  "Her iron pills are not working because the first part of her small intestine, where iron is soaked up, is damaged. The rash and stomach trouble point to gluten causing that damage, and a blood test checks for it first.",
  exim="fig_celiac_duodenum",
  excap="Normal duodenum with tall villi (left) compared with celiac disease showing villous atrophy (right).")

# ── 20. Proton pump inhibitors and B12 (§5) ───────────────────────────────
q("A 72-year-old woman comes to the office because of a 6-month history of fatigue and tingling in both feet. For the past 11 years she has taken omeprazole 40 mg daily for gastroesophageal reflux disease. She eats a diet that includes meat and dairy, does not drink alcoholic beverages, and has had no abdominal surgery. Serum anti-intrinsic factor and anti-parietal cell antibodies are absent.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 10.8 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 32% (N=36%–46%)\nMean corpuscular volume 108 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nSerum vitamin B12 168 pg/mL (N=200–900 pg/mL)\nSerum folate 9.8 ng/mL (N=2.5–20 ng/mL)\nMethylmalonic acid 0.74 µmol/L (N=0.08–0.40 µmol/L)\n\nImpaired vitamin B12 absorption in this patient most likely results from failure at which of the following steps?",
  ["A. Binding of vitamin B12 to intrinsic factor in the duodenum", "B. Release of vitamin B12 from dietary protein in the stomach", "C. Secretion of intrinsic factor by gastric parietal cells", "D. Transfer of vitamin B12 to transcobalamin II in the plasma", "E. Uptake of the vitamin B12–intrinsic factor complex by ileal cubilin receptors"],
  1,
  "The review file walks vitamin B12 along a long road with many exits. The first station is in the STOMACH: dietary B12 arrives bound to food protein, and GASTRIC ACID and PEPSIN are what liberate it so it can be picked up by haptocorrin and later intrinsic factor.\n\nA proton pump inhibitor removes acid → pepsinogen is not activated → B12 is NEVER RELEASED from food protein → it passes through unabsorbed. The review file lists proton pump inhibitors by name among drug-induced causes, with exactly this mechanism. After 11 years of daily omeprazole she has a macrocytic anemia, a low B12 and a raised methylmalonic acid, with antibodies ruling out pernicious anemia.\n\nThis is the trap: parietal cells make BOTH acid and intrinsic factor — the review file tells you to remember that pairing. A proton pump inhibitor blocks the acid pump but leaves intrinsic factor secretion intact. The failure is upstream of intrinsic factor, at the release step.\n\nA practical corollary: crystalline B12 in supplements is not protein-bound, so it does not need acid to be released and is absorbed normally in these patients.",
  {"0": "Binding to intrinsic factor in the duodenum requires pancreatic proteases to cleave haptocorrin first. It fails in pancreatic insufficiency — chronic pancreatitis or cystic fibrosis — which nothing in her history suggests.",
   "2": "Loss of intrinsic factor secretion is pernicious anemia or gastrectomy. The shared parietal cell makes this the near-miss, but proton pump inhibitors block the acid pump, not intrinsic factor, and her antibodies are absent.",
   "3": "Transcobalamin II carries B12 in the plasma AFTER absorption. Congenital deficiency presents in infancy; acid suppression has no effect on it.",
   "4": "Ileal uptake through cubilin fails with terminal ileal resection or Crohn disease, and metformin interferes with this calcium-dependent step. She has no ileal disease and takes no metformin."},
  "Vitamin B12 in food is stuck to protein, and stomach acid is what pries it loose. Her acid-blocking pill stops that from happening, so the vitamin stays stuck and passes straight through.",
  exim="fig_b12_absorption",
  excap="Vitamin B12 absorption: HCl and pepsin release cobalamin from food protein in the stomach; later steps require intrinsic factor, pancreatic trypsin, ileal cubilin and transcobalamin II.")

# ── 21. Fish tapeworm (§5) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
q("A 45-year-old man comes to the office because of a 5-month history of fatigue and numbness in his hands. He lives near Lake Superior and frequently eats pike and perch that he catches, cures, and eats raw. He has occasionally noticed flat, ribbon-like segments in his stool. He eats a diet that includes meat and dairy, does not drink alcoholic beverages, and has had no abdominal surgery. Serum anti-intrinsic factor antibodies are absent.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 10.9 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\nHematocrit 33% (N=41%–53%)\nMean corpuscular volume 110 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nSerum vitamin B12 140 pg/mL (N=200–900 pg/mL)\nSerum folate 12.0 ng/mL (N=2.5–20 ng/mL)\nMethylmalonic acid 0.81 µmol/L (N=0.08–0.40 µmol/L)\n\nWhich of the following infectious agents is the most likely cause of this patient's vitamin B12 deficiency?",
  ["A. Ascaris lumbricoides", "B. Diphyllobothrium latum", "C. Giardia lamblia", "D. Necator americanus", "E. Taenia solium"],
  1,
  "Vitamin B12 deficiency with an intact stomach, no ileal disease, an adequate diet and absent antibodies needs another explanation. The review file lists COMPETITIVE UPTAKE as a cause — something in the gut consumes the B12 before the patient can absorb it.\n\nThe fish tapeworm, DIPHYLLOBOTHRIUM LATUM, is acquired by eating RAW or undercooked FRESHWATER FISH containing larvae → the adult worm lives in the small intestine, can grow to several meters, and sheds flat ribbon-like proglottids in the stool → it absorbs vitamin B12 avidly, competing with the host → megaloblastic anemia and, eventually, neurologic signs.\n\nThe two buried clues are the raw cured freshwater fish from the Great Lakes region and the ribbon-like segments. The review file's other competitive-uptake cause is intestinal bacterial overgrowth, which would be suggested by blind loops, strictures or dysmotility. Treatment is praziquantel with B12 replacement.",
  {"0": "Ascaris is a roundworm acquired from fecally contaminated soil. Heavy infection causes intestinal or biliary obstruction and malnutrition in children, and the passed worms are round and earthworm-like, not flat segments.",
   "2": "Giardia is acquired from contaminated water and causes foul-smelling fatty diarrhea and malabsorption of fats and fat-soluble vitamins. It is a protozoan, so no worm segments would be seen, and B12 deficiency is not its characteristic consequence.",
   "3": "Necator americanus, a hookworm, penetrates the skin from soil and feeds on blood in the small intestine. It causes IRON deficiency — a microcytic anemia — which is the opposite of this patient's macrocytosis.",
   "4": "Taenia solium, the pork tapeworm, is also a flat worm that sheds segments, which makes it the near-miss. It is acquired from undercooked pork, its danger is cysticercosis of the brain and muscle, and it does not cause B12 deficiency."},
  "Eating raw lake fish gave him a very long tapeworm living in his gut. The worm eats up the vitamin B12 from his food before his body can absorb it.")

# ── 22. The Schilling test localizes the lesion (§5) ──────────────────────
q("A 59-year-old man is evaluated for a macrocytic anemia with a serum vitamin B12 concentration of 96 pg/mL (N=200–900 pg/mL). A Schilling test is performed. An intramuscular injection of unlabeled vitamin B12 is first given to saturate body binding sites, so that any absorbed oral B12 is excreted in the urine. He then takes an oral dose of radiolabeled vitamin B12, and urine is collected for 24 hours; only 1% of the radiolabeled dose is recovered (N=>8%). One week later, the test is repeated with the oral radiolabeled vitamin B12 given together with exogenous intrinsic factor, and 15% of the dose is recovered in the urine.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's vitamin B12 deficiency?",
  ["A. Autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells", "B. Bacterial overgrowth in the small intestine", "C. Chronic pancreatitis with exocrine insufficiency", "D. Inadequate dietary intake of animal products", "E. Resection of the terminal ileum"],
  0,
  "The Schilling test is no longer performed, but the review file keeps it because it localizes a B12 absorption defect better than any single modern test — it teaches the anatomy.\n\nThe logic:\n• The unlabeled injection saturates binding sites → any radiolabeled B12 that is ABSORBED spills into the urine.\n• STAGE I (oral B12 alone): 1% recovered → the oral dose was NOT absorbed. That already excludes a purely dietary cause, where absorption is normal.\n• STAGE II (oral B12 plus intrinsic factor): excretion NORMALIZES to 15% → supplying intrinsic factor fixed the problem → the missing ingredient was INTRINSIC FACTOR → PERNICIOUS ANEMIA.\n\nIf stage II had still been abnormal, the defect would lie beyond intrinsic factor — at the terminal ileum (ileitis, resection) or in the lumen (bacterial overgrowth, fish tapeworm). Pernicious anemia is autoimmune destruction of the gastric mucosa with loss of the parietal cells that make intrinsic factor.",
  {"1": "Bacterial overgrowth consumes B12 in the lumen, so adding intrinsic factor does NOT correct stage II. Excretion would normalize only after a course of antibiotics — the historical 'stage III'.",
   "2": "Pancreatic insufficiency prevents cleavage of haptocorrin, so B12 cannot transfer to intrinsic factor. Stage II would not correct with intrinsic factor alone; it corrects when pancreatic enzymes are given.",
   "3": "Dietary deficiency, as in a strict vegan, leaves every absorptive step intact. Stage I would therefore be NORMAL — the oral radiolabeled dose would be absorbed and excreted.",
   "4": "Terminal ileal resection removes the cubilin receptors that take up the B12–intrinsic factor complex. Adding intrinsic factor cannot help when there is nowhere to absorb the complex, so stage II would remain abnormal."},
  "When they gave the missing helper protein from the stomach along with the vitamin, he absorbed it fine. That means the only thing wrong was his stomach not making that helper.")

# ── 23. Pernicious anemia antibodies — sensitive versus specific (§5) ─────
q("A 63-year-old woman is evaluated for a macrocytic anemia. She has a history of type 1 diabetes mellitus and primary adrenal insufficiency. She eats a diet that includes meat and dairy and has had no gastrointestinal surgery. Neurologic examination shows decreased vibration sense in the toes.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 9.1 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 27% (N=36%–46%)\nMean corpuscular volume 116 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nSerum vitamin B12 110 pg/mL (N=200–900 pg/mL)\nMethylmalonic acid 0.96 µmol/L (N=0.08–0.40 µmol/L)\n\nPernicious anemia is suspected.\n\nWhich of the following laboratory findings is most specific for this diagnosis?",
  ["A. Anti-intrinsic factor antibodies", "B. Anti-parietal cell antibodies", "C. Anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies", "D. Elevated serum gastrin concentration", "E. Elevated serum homocysteine concentration"],
  0,
  "The review file gives the two pernicious anemia antibodies as a pair with opposite strengths:\n• ANTI-PARIETAL CELL antibody — present in 85–90% → MORE SENSITIVE, but also found in other autoimmune gastritis and in a proportion of healthy older adults.\n• ANTI-INTRINSIC FACTOR antibody — present in about 75% → MORE SPECIFIC. When it is present, pernicious anemia is very likely.\n\nSo the lead-in 'most specific' points to anti-intrinsic factor antibodies, while a question asking for the most sensitive screen would point to anti-parietal cell antibodies.\n\nThe stem supports the diagnosis in other ways too. Pernicious anemia is an autoimmune disease, not a diet problem, and it clusters with other autoimmune endocrine disease — here type 1 diabetes and primary adrenal insufficiency. The B12 deficiency is confirmed by a raised methylmalonic acid, and early dorsal column involvement shows as reduced vibration sense.",
  {"1": "Anti-parietal cell antibodies are the MORE SENSITIVE of the two, found in 85–90% of patients. Their weakness is specificity — they also occur in other atrophic gastritis and in some healthy older adults — which makes them the near-miss for a 'most specific' lead-in.",
   "2": "Anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies mark Hashimoto thyroiditis, which often coexists with pernicious anemia as part of the same autoimmune cluster. They say nothing specific about the stomach.",
   "3": "Gastrin rises because loss of acid removes the negative feedback on antral G cells, so hypergastrinemia is expected in pernicious anemia. It is also raised by proton pump inhibitors, gastrinoma and other causes of achlorhydria, so it is not specific.",
   "4": "Homocysteine rises in both B12 and folate deficiency, and in renal failure and hypothyroidism. It cannot identify the cause of the B12 deficiency, let alone pernicious anemia specifically."},
  "One antibody test catches almost everyone with this condition but also some people without it. The other misses a few people but, when it is positive, it is almost always this condition.")

# ── 24. Methotrexate — the folate antagonist (§5) ─────────────────────────
q("A 64-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis comes to the office because of painful mouth sores and fatigue for 1 week. She has taken methotrexate 20 mg orally once weekly for 3 years, without folic acid supplementation. Ten days ago she began trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for a urinary tract infection. Examination shows multiple shallow ulcers on the buccal mucosa and tongue.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 8.8 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 26% (N=36%–46%)\nMean corpuscular volume 109 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nLeukocyte count 2,300/mm3 (N=4,500–11,000/mm3)\nPlatelet count 74,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\nSerum vitamin B12 540 pg/mL (N=200–900 pg/mL)\nSerum creatinine 1.0 mg/dL (N=0.6–1.2 mg/dL)\n\nA peripheral blood smear shows oval macrocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy to reverse these adverse effects?",
  ["A. Cyanocobalamin", "B. Filgrastim", "C. Folic acid", "D. Leucovorin", "E. Pyridoxine"],
  3,
  "The review file lists IMPAIRED UTILIZATION of folate among the causes of folate deficiency, naming the folate antagonist METHOTREXATE, and explains why chemotherapy targets this pathway at all. Chemotherapy is broadly a poison aimed at fast-dividing cells → fast-dividing cells are busy with DNA synthesis → shut down the folate pathway → the side effects are predictable, because the fastest-dividing normal tissues suffer first: the MARROW empties (pancytopenia) and the GUT MUCOSA ulcerates (her stomatitis).\n\nMechanism: methotrexate inhibits DIHYDROFOLATE REDUCTASE → dihydrofolate cannot be reduced to tetrahydrofolate → no one-carbon donors for thymidylate → DNA synthesis stalls → megaloblastic pancytopenia. Trimethoprim is a weaker dihydrofolate reductase inhibitor, and it also reduces renal clearance of methotrexate, which is why the combination precipitated toxicity.\n\nThe reversal agent is LEUCOVORIN (folinic acid, N5-formyl-tetrahydrofolate). It is ALREADY REDUCED, so it enters the pathway BELOW the blocked enzyme and restores tetrahydrofolate even while dihydrofolate reductase is inhibited. The trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole should be stopped and methotrexate held.",
  {"0": "Cyanocobalamin treats vitamin B12 deficiency. Her B12 is normal at 540 pg/mL, and the megaloblastic change here comes from blocked folate metabolism rather than a missing cofactor.",
   "1": "Filgrastim shortens neutropenia by stimulating granulocyte production, and may be added in severe neutropenia. It does not correct the underlying DNA synthesis block, the anemia, the thrombocytopenia or the mucositis.",
   "2": "Folic acid is the near-miss. Ordinary folic acid must be reduced by DIHYDROFOLATE REDUCTASE to become active — the very enzyme methotrexate is blocking — so it cannot rescue acute toxicity. Low-dose folic acid is, however, given routinely with weekly methotrexate to PREVENT these effects.",
   "4": "Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) is the cofactor for heme synthesis and treats sideroblastic anemia, particularly isoniazid-induced. It has no role in folate metabolism."},
  "Her arthritis drug blocks the enzyme that turns folate into its usable form, so fast-growing cells in the marrow and mouth stop dividing properly. Leucovorin is folate that is already in usable form, so it slips past the blocked enzyme.",
  exim="fig_mtx_pathway",
  excap="Methotrexate inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHF → THF); folinic acid (leucovorin) enters as a reduced folate below the block.")

# ── 25. Chronic hemolysis consumes folate (§5) ────────────────────────────
q("A 16-year-old boy with hereditary spherocytosis is brought to the office because of progressively worsening fatigue over 6 weeks. His baseline hemoglobin concentration is 11 g/dL, with a reticulocyte count of 8%. He has had no fever, rash, sore throat, or recent illness. He eats very few fruits or vegetables. His pulse is 108/min. Examination shows pallor, mild scleral icterus, and a spleen palpable 3 cm below the left costal margin, unchanged from previous examinations.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 7.2 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\nHematocrit 22% (N=41%–53%)\nMean corpuscular volume 104 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nReticulocyte count 1.1% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\nSerum vitamin B12 520 pg/mL (N=200–900 pg/mL)\nSerum folate 1.6 ng/mL (N=2.5–20 ng/mL)\n\nA test for parvovirus B19 IgM is negative. A peripheral blood smear is shown; hypersegmented neutrophils are also present elsewhere on the slide.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely cause of the worsening of this patient's anemia?",
  ["A. Aplastic crisis from parvovirus B19 infection", "B. Folate deficiency from increased requirement", "C. Hyperhemolytic crisis with splenic sequestration", "D. Iron deficiency from chronic hemolysis", "E. Vitamin B12 deficiency from ileal malabsorption"],
  1,
  "A patient with chronic hemolysis suddenly stops making enough red cells — his reticulocyte count has collapsed from a baseline of 8% to 1.1%. Two causes produce that, and they are separated by tempo and morphology.\n\nThe review file lists CHRONIC HEMOLYSIS as a cause of INCREASED FOLATE REQUIREMENT. His marrow has been running at several times normal output for years to compensate for spherocyte destruction → every new cell needs folate for DNA synthesis → a poor intake of fruits and vegetables cannot keep up → folate runs out → megaloblastic erythropoiesis.\n\nThe discriminators:\n• MCV has RISEN to 104 µm3 — spherocytosis alone would keep it normal or low.\n• HYPERSEGMENTED NEUTROPHILS — a DNA synthesis defect.\n• Serum folate 1.6 ng/mL with a normal B12.\n• A GRADUAL 6-week decline.\n\nThis is why patients with chronic hemolytic anemias are given daily folic acid supplementation.",
  {"0": "Aplastic crisis is the near-miss: parvovirus B19 infects erythroid precursors and also drops the reticulocyte count abruptly in chronic hemolysis. It comes on over days, usually after a viral illness, and gives a NORMOCYTIC picture without hypersegmented neutrophils. His IgM is negative and his course is gradual and macrocytic.",
   "2": "A hyperhemolytic or sequestration crisis would drive the reticulocyte count UP, with rapid enlargement of the spleen and worsening jaundice. His reticulocyte count has fallen and his spleen is unchanged.",
   "3": "Extravascular hemolysis in the spleen RECYCLES iron through macrophages, so iron deficiency does not develop. Iron deficiency would also produce microcytosis, not an MCV of 104 µm3.",
   "4": "Vitamin B12 deficiency would produce the same smear, but his serum B12 is normal at 520 pg/mL and he has no ileal disease. The body's B12 stores last years, whereas folate stores last only months — which is why folate is the vitamin a high-turnover marrow exhausts."},
  "His marrow has to work overtime to replace the fragile red cells he keeps losing, and building all those cells uses up folate. He did not eat enough folate to keep up, so the marrow ran out of supplies.",
  image="fig_spherocytes",
  imcap="Peripheral blood smear. Many erythrocytes are small, round and densely staining, without central pallor; cell size varies.",
  exim="fig_parvo_pronormoblast",
  excap="The contrast case: a giant pronormoblast with deep-blue cytoplasm, the marrow hallmark of parvovirus B19 aplastic crisis.")

# ── 26. Two deficiencies, one normal MCV (§5) ─────────────────────────────
q("A 41-year-old woman comes to the office because of a 4-month history of fatigue and numbness in both feet. Six years ago she underwent Roux-en-Y gastric bypass. She stopped taking her prescribed vitamin supplements 3 years ago. Her menstrual periods are regular and light. Neurologic examination shows decreased vibration sense in the toes.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 9.0 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 27% (N=36%–46%)\nMean corpuscular volume 88 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nRed cell distribution width 23.4% (N=11.5%–14.5%)\nSerum ferritin 5 ng/mL (N=10–120 ng/mL)\nSerum vitamin B12 118 pg/mL (N=200–900 pg/mL)\nSerum folate 7.0 ng/mL (N=2.5–20 ng/mL)\nMethylmalonic acid 0.90 µmol/L (N=0.08–0.40 µmol/L)\n\nA peripheral blood smear shows a mixture of small, pale erythrocytes and large, oval erythrocytes, with occasional hypersegmented neutrophils.\n\nWhich of the following best explains this patient's mean corpuscular volume?",
  ["A. Anemia of chronic disease producing normocytic erythrocytes", "B. Coexisting iron and vitamin B12 deficiencies producing two erythrocyte populations", "C. Early deficiency before erythrocyte size has changed", "D. Reticulocytosis offsetting microcytosis", "E. Spurious elevation from erythrocyte agglutination"],
  1,
  "A normal MCV with a red cell distribution width of 23.4% is a warning, not a reassurance. The MCV is an AVERAGE, and an average of two abnormal populations can be normal.\n\nThe review file names bariatric surgery as a modern, common cause of exactly this. Gastric bypass removes or bypasses the acid- and intrinsic-factor-producing stomach AND the duodenum → a predictable long-term deficiency of IRON (lost duodenal absorption, less acid) and VITAMIN B12 (no intrinsic factor, no acid to release B12 from food) TOGETHER → it is 'a common source of the mixed micro-plus-macrocytic picture that averages out to a normal MCV.'\n\nEverything confirms two separate deficiencies: a ferritin of 5 ng/mL (iron), a low B12 with a raised methylmalonic acid (B12), neuropathy, and a smear showing both populations — small pale cells AND oval macrocytes with hypersegmented neutrophils. The wide red cell distribution width is the index that betrays the mixture. Both deficiencies need replacement, with lifelong monitoring after bariatric surgery.",
  {"0": "Anemia of chronic disease is normocytic, but it has a NORMAL red cell distribution width, a normal or high ferritin, and an inflammatory cause. Her ferritin is 5 ng/mL and her smear shows two distinct abnormal populations rather than uniform cells.",
   "2": "Early deficiency before cells change size would give a normal MCV with a NORMAL red cell distribution width and a normal smear. A width of 23.4% with visibly small and large cells means the size change has already happened.",
   "3": "Reticulocytes are larger than mature cells, so a brisk reticulocytosis can pull a low MCV up toward normal. It needs an active marrow responding to loss or hemolysis, which a combined nutritional production defect does not provide.",
   "4": "Agglutination from cold antibodies spuriously RAISES the MCV as clumps are counted as single large cells. It would be seen as clumping on the smear, which is not described, and would not explain the iron and B12 results."},
  "She is short on two nutrients at once: one makes red cells small and the other makes them big. Averaged together the size looks normal, but the smear shows both kinds.",
  exim="fig_intestinal_absorption",
  excap="Absorptive capacity along the intestine: vitamin B12 is taken up in the ileum. Iron, not shown, is absorbed in the duodenum — the segment gastric bypass skips.")

# ── 27. Oral B12 in pernicious anemia — passive diffusion (§5) ────────────
q("A 68-year-old man with pernicious anemia confirmed by anti-intrinsic factor antibodies has received monthly intramuscular cyanocobalamin injections for 2 years. He has a severe needle phobia and asks whether he can take tablets instead. His physician prescribes oral cyanocobalamin 1,000 µg daily. Three months later, his hemoglobin concentration, serum vitamin B12 concentration, and methylmalonic acid concentration are all within the reference ranges.\n\nThe effectiveness of this oral regimen depends on which of the following?",
  ["A. Increased gastric acid release of vitamin B12 from the tablet matrix", "B. Passive diffusion of a small fraction of a large dose independent of intrinsic factor", "C. Recovery of intrinsic factor secretion after vitamin B12 repletion", "D. Transcobalamin II–mediated uptake across the gastric mucosa", "E. Up-regulation of ileal cubilin receptors in vitamin B12 deficiency"],
  1,
  "In pernicious anemia there is no intrinsic factor, so the normal pathway — the intrinsic factor–B12 complex taken up by cubilin in the terminal ileum — is permanently closed.\n\nThe review file states the treatment rule directly: B12 must be given by a route that does NOT require intrinsic factor. Two routes qualify:\n• INTRAMUSCULAR injection — bypasses the gut completely.\n• HIGH-DOSE ORAL therapy — relies on PASSIVE DIFFUSION across the intestinal mucosa.\n\nPassive diffusion absorbs only about 1% of an oral dose regardless of intrinsic factor. That is why the dose is enormous: 1% of 1,000 µg is roughly 10 µg a day, comfortably above the daily requirement of a few micrograms. The normal-range B12 and methylmalonic acid confirm it is working.\n\nThe review file also reminds you that treating pernicious anemia means treating three problems: the anemia and the neurology are covered by adequate B12 by either route, but the atrophic gastritis and its gastric carcinoma risk still need surveillance.",
  {"0": "Pernicious anemia causes ACHLORHYDRIA — the same destroyed parietal cells make acid. And the crystalline B12 in tablets is not protein-bound, so it needs no acid release in any case.",
   "2": "Pernicious anemia is autoimmune destruction of parietal cells, which is permanent. Repleting B12 does not regenerate them or restore intrinsic factor, which is why treatment is lifelong.",
   "3": "Transcobalamin II carries B12 in the plasma after absorption and delivers it to tissues. It plays no role in moving B12 across the gut, and the stomach is not an absorptive site.",
   "4": "Cubilin receptors take up only the intrinsic factor–B12 COMPLEX. With no intrinsic factor, more receptors would have nothing to bind."},
  "Normally the body needs a helper protein to absorb B12, and his stomach cannot make it. But if you swallow a very large dose, a tiny bit leaks through the gut wall on its own — and a tiny bit of a big dose is enough.")

# ── 28. Anemia of chronic disease — erythropoietin is blunted (§6) ────────
q("A 57-year-old man is evaluated for fatigue. For 3 months he has been treated for a lung abscess and still has a productive cough and intermittent fevers. He has no history of kidney disease and has never received erythropoiesis-stimulating agents. He is breathing room air. His temperature is 37.9°C (100.2°F), pulse is 92/min, and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 95%.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 9.4 g/dL (N=13.5–17.5 g/dL)\nHematocrit 28% (N=41%–53%)\nMean corpuscular volume 85 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nReticulocyte count 0.6% (N=0.5%–1.5%)\nSerum iron 30 µg/dL (N=65–175 µg/dL)\nSerum ferritin 380 ng/mL (N=20–250 ng/mL)\nTotal iron-binding capacity 205 µg/dL (N=250–400 µg/dL)\nSerum creatinine 0.9 mg/dL (N=0.6–1.2 mg/dL)\nSerum erythropoietin 22 mU/mL (N=4–26 mU/mL)\n\nWhich of the following best explains this patient's serum erythropoietin concentration?",
  ["A. Destruction of renal peritubular interstitial cells", "B. Hepcidin-mediated sequestration of iron in macrophages", "C. Inflammatory cytokine suppression of renal erythropoietin release", "D. Neutralizing antibodies against endogenous erythropoietin", "E. Suppression of hypoxia-inducible factor by supplemental oxygen"],
  2,
  "An erythropoietin of 22 mU/mL looks 'normal' — and that is the abnormality. A healthy kidney facing a hemoglobin of 9.4 g/dL should raise erythropoietin many-fold above the reference range. A normal value in the face of anemia is INAPPROPRIATELY LOW.\n\nThe review file frames anemia of chronic disease as FOUR MECHANISMS, ONE STIMULUS — the inflammatory cytokine response. Hepcidin is the famous one, but it is only the first:\nA. Hepcidin goes up → iron is locked away.\nB. ERYTHROPOIETIN RELEASE IS INHIBITED → 'the order is never given loudly enough.'\nC. Erythroid proliferation is inhibited directly by cytokines.\nD. Hemophagocytosis is augmented.\n\nThe lead-in asks specifically about the erythropoietin number, and mechanism B is the one that explains it. The stem removes the alternatives: a normal creatinine excludes kidney disease, he has never had an erythropoiesis-stimulating agent, and he is breathing room air.",
  {"0": "Loss of the peritubular interstitial cells that make erythropoietin is the anemia of CHRONIC KIDNEY DISEASE. It would be correct with an elevated creatinine; his creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL.",
   "1": "Hepcidin-mediated iron sequestration is genuinely happening in this patient — it explains his low serum iron, high ferritin and low binding capacity. It is the right disease but the wrong mechanism for this lead-in, because hepcidin controls iron, not erythropoietin.",
   "3": "Anti-erythropoietin antibodies cause pure red cell aplasia, almost exclusively after treatment with recombinant erythropoiesis-stimulating agents. He has never received one, and he would have profound reticulocytopenia with an absent erythroid series.",
   "4": "Supplemental oxygen raises tissue oxygen, allowing prolyl hydroxylation and degradation of hypoxia-inducible factor, which lowers erythropoietin. He is breathing room air, so this cannot apply."},
  "Anemia normally makes the kidneys shout for more red cells. His ongoing infection floods his body with chemicals that turn that shout into a whisper, so the hormone level sits at 'normal' when it should be sky-high.")

# ── 29. Why the TIBC is low in inflammation (§6) ──────────────────────────
q("A 66-year-old woman is in week 6 of intravenous antibiotic therapy for infective endocarditis of the mitral valve. She reports fatigue. She has no history of liver disease and does not drink alcoholic beverages. A urinalysis shows no protein.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 9.9 g/dL (N=12.0–16.0 g/dL)\nHematocrit 30% (N=36%–46%)\nMean corpuscular volume 84 µm3 (N=80–100 µm3)\nSerum iron 28 µg/dL (N=50–170 µg/dL)\nSerum ferritin 290 ng/mL (N=10–120 ng/mL)\nTotal iron-binding capacity 196 µg/dL (N=250–400 µg/dL)\nSerum albumin 3.6 g/dL (N=3.5–5.5 g/dL)\nAspartate aminotransferase 24 U/L (N=12–38 U/L)\n\nWhich of the following best explains this patient's decreased total iron-binding capacity?",
  ["A. Increased occupancy of transferrin binding sites by iron", "B. Increased renal loss of transferrin", "C. Increased uptake of transferrin by erythroid precursors", "D. Reduced hepatic synthesis of transferrin as a negative acute phase reactant", "E. Sequestration of transferrin within macrophages by hepcidin"],
  3,
  "Total iron-binding capacity is an indirect measure of TRANSFERRIN — how many iron-binding sites exist in the plasma. The review file's picture is transferrin carrying little cups, full or empty.\n\nIn iron deficiency the cups float around empty and the liver makes MORE transferrin → HIGH binding capacity. In anemia of chronic disease the opposite happens: inflammation drives the liver to make LESS transferrin, because transferrin is a NEGATIVE ACUTE PHASE REACTANT — its production falls as acute phase proteins rise → fewer cups on the shelf → LOW binding capacity.\n\nThat single difference, the review file says, separates the two most commonly confused anemias in the hospital: ferritin and total iron-binding capacity move in OPPOSITE directions — iron deficiency LOW/HIGH, anemia of chronic disease HIGH/LOW.\n\nThe stem rules out the other causes of low transferrin: no proteinuria, no liver disease, and a preserved albumin.",
  {"0": "Iron occupying the sites would raise the transferrin SATURATION, not lower the total capacity — capacity counts all sites, full or empty. And her serum iron is low, so there is less iron to occupy them.",
   "1": "Urinary transferrin loss lowers the binding capacity in NEPHROTIC SYNDROME, along with heavy proteinuria and a low albumin. Her urinalysis shows no protein and her albumin is 3.6 g/dL.",
   "2": "Erythroid precursors take up transferrin-bound iron through transferrin receptors, but the transferrin itself is recycled back to the plasma. Uptake does not deplete the circulating transferrin pool, and her marrow is under-producing, not over-consuming.",
   "4": "Hepcidin acts on FERROPORTIN, the iron exporter, trapping IRON inside macrophages. It does not sequester transferrin — this is the right hormone applied to the wrong molecule."},
  "The blood protein that carries iron is made in the liver. During a long infection the liver deliberately makes less of it, so there are fewer iron carriers in the blood.")

# ── 30. The vegan mother and the breastfed infant (§5) ────────────────────
q("A 10-month-old girl is brought to the office because of lethargy, poor feeding, and loss of the ability to sit without support over the past 2 months; she first sat unsupported at 6 months of age. She was born at term after an uncomplicated pregnancy and has been exclusively breastfed. Her mother has followed a strict vegan diet for 6 years and takes no supplements. Examination shows pallor, generalized hypotonia, and decreased spontaneous movements.\n\nLaboratory studies show:\nHemoglobin 8.4 g/dL (N=10.5–13.5 g/dL for age)\nHematocrit 25% (N=33%–39% for age)\nMean corpuscular volume 101 µm3 (N=70–86 µm3 for age)\nPlatelet count 128,000/mm3 (N=150,000–400,000/mm3)\nSerum vitamin B12 84 pg/mL (N=200–900 pg/mL)\nSerum folate 14.0 ng/mL (N=2.5–20 ng/mL)\nMethylmalonic acid 3.2 µmol/L (N=0.08–0.40 µmol/L)\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely underlying cause of this patient's condition?",
  ["A. Congenital transcobalamin II deficiency", "B. Folate deficiency from goat's milk feeding", "C. Hereditary intrinsic factor deficiency", "D. Iron deficiency from excess cow's milk intake", "E. Maternal dietary vitamin B12 deficiency"],
  4,
  "The review file names two relevant facts: the major source of B12 is ANIMAL PRODUCTS — plants contain very little, which is why STRICT VEGANS are at genuine risk — and B12 has TWO JOBS, DNA synthesis and maintaining myelin.\n\nPut them together across two people. A mother on a strict vegan diet for 6 years without supplements has low B12 stores → her breast milk contains little B12 → her exclusively breastfed infant, born with low stores, depletes them over the first months → megaloblastic anemia (macrocytosis, low platelets) PLUS the neurologic job failing in a developing brain → lethargy, hypotonia and loss of a milestone already achieved. The very high methylmalonic acid confirms B12 deficiency, and the normal folate excludes folate.\n\nDevelopmental REGRESSION in an exclusively breastfed infant of a vegan mother is the vignette to recognize. Treatment is B12 for both mother and infant; neurologic recovery may be incomplete if it is delayed.",
  {"0": "Congenital transcobalamin II deficiency presents in the FIRST FEW MONTHS of life, and the serum B12 is often NORMAL because B12 is carried on other binders while delivery to cells fails. This infant developed normally until after 6 months and has a very low serum B12.",
   "1": "Goat's milk is genuinely deficient in folate and causes megaloblastic anemia in infants fed on it — a classic distractor. She is exclusively breastfed, her folate is normal, and folate deficiency would not raise methylmalonic acid or cause neurologic regression.",
   "2": "Hereditary intrinsic factor deficiency does cause infantile B12 deficiency with neurologic signs, typically after maternal stores run out. It becomes the leading answer only when the mother's own B12 status is adequate — here the mother's strict unsupplemented vegan diet provides a far more likely explanation.",
   "3": "Excess cow's milk causes IRON deficiency in toddlers, with MICROcytosis. She is exclusively breastfed, her MCV is high, and iron deficiency does not raise methylmalonic acid."},
  "Vitamin B12 comes almost only from animal foods. Her mom eats none and takes no vitamins, so there was very little B12 in the breast milk, and the baby's growing brain and blood both ran short.",
  exim="fig_b12_def_uw",
  excap="Vitamin B12 deficiency: methylmalonyl-CoA mutase failure raises methylmalonic acid and impairs myelin; methionine synthase failure traps folate and impairs DNA synthesis.")
